  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542",
    "id": "q-118552",
    "status": "supported",
    "reason": null,
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-118552",
      "knowledge_point_id": "4728b3c6-a600-569d-922e-228e9f6fb2a6",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model In Context",
      "problem": "The population $y(t)$ of Weddell seals in a certain region of Antarctica follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1.2y-0.002{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=100,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years. According to this model, what is the maximum possible seal population?",
      "worked_solution": "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1.2y-0.002{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.002y(600-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=600.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $600,$ and we conclude that the number of seals cannot exceed $600.$",
